  "notes": "Measured 2026-11-24 for Skyarium's ghost flights. The 737-200 has no measured shape, so the scheme is drawn on the 737-300 (B733): the 737-200's nose and forward body are the same, and its paint is placed in meters from the nose (the title 7.2-14.7 m back). Every straight paint line is one height in meters along the body: the band 0.10-0.74 m and the belly line -0.60 m above the centerline, read off N803N against the crown and belly at the same station (the camera about level) and checked on N753N and N794N; the points are where the shape's outline bends. At the nose the band's lower edge eases down in a gentle curve from 5.6 m back to the nose tip, and the belly line curves down to meet the chin about 0.6 m back. Where the crown dips under the band's top just ahead of the windshield the band covers the nose. At the tail both run straight out through the tail cone's underside. The fin's speedbird and title were placed in fin space on N794N (right side, its fin edges segmented against the sky) and N753N (left side); they agree within a few percent of the chord. Colors are from faded slides and only approximate. On the 737-300s the fin also carried '737-300' between blue pinstripes (N301P, 1985), not drawn. Piedmont Aviation (1948-1989) merged into USAir in August 1989; it's keyed PI1948 because its old code now belongs to a different, living airline (PDT). No US flag shows on the 1983-88 photos; each jet's name (N783N's 'Seminole Pacemaker') is lettered small in blue under the cockpit and isn't drawn. The scheme was already on the 737s in 1975 (N738N); when it came in isn't dated here.",
  "types": {
    "B722": {
      "note": "The 727's band and belly line sit a little higher on its deeper body (0.17-0.81 m and -0.53 m above its centerline) and run out through the tail cone; the title is a little bigger, and the fin's marks sit low on the 727's short fin, as on N555PS (1984). There the title is lettered on the center intake's fairing, its baseline about 0.7 m under the fin's leading-edge foot; fin lettering is drawn clipped to the fin above its root, so it's raised to just above the root here, with the speedbird moved up as far and made about a tenth smaller to stay under the tailplane. Refitted 2026-11-24 on the passenger 727 (shapes/B722-passenger.json): the band 0.66 m tall with the windows in its middle (N555PS), the belly's line 0.98 m under it, both level on the cabin.",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "sourceRefs": [
        3,
        4
      ],
